api_client.py

    fetch_json(url)
    Fetches JSON from a URL and converts it into a Python dictionary. If the internet is down, the API returns an error, or the JSON is broken, it returns None so the program does not crash.

    get_timezone(city, country_code)
    Asks the Open-Meteo geocoding API for the city and returns the time zone, for example "Europe/Berlin". The user only enters city and country; coordinates stay hidden internally.

    get_holidays(country_code, year)
    Asks the Nager.Date API for public holidays in a country for a given year and returns a list of date strings. Used to exclude days from planning.

main.py

    main()
    Starts the program, calls all other functions in the right order, and displays the result. Lives behind the if __name__ == "__main__": guard.

    ask_input()
    Prompts the user for all inputs like country, city, date, and blocked times, and stores everything in a dictionary. Ensures the data is available consistently throughout the program.

    parse_slots(raw_slots)
    Takes the raw blocked-time strings from the user and splits them into usable parts like date, start time, and end time. This allows later calculations instead of working with plain text.

    to_utc(local_datetime, timezone)
    Converts a local date and time with a time zone into UTC so both people become comparable. UTC is the common language for comparing times across time zones.

    is_free(utc_time, blocked_slots_utc)
    Checks whether a given UTC moment falls inside a blocked window. Returns True if the slot is free and False if it is occupied.

    find_common_slots(date, tz_a, tz_b, busy_a, busy_b, holidays)
    Steps through possible meeting times and finds hours where both people are working and not blocked. Holidays are filtered out too.

    format_output(slot, tz_a, tz_b)
    Takes a found UTC meeting slot and writes it so both users see their local time. For example: "09:00 Berlin / 02:00 Chicago".

    save_result(result, filename)
    Writes the final result to a file, for example as text or JSON. This keeps the result saved after the program ends.
